---
import Inferred from "../../components/Inferred.astro";
import Layer from "../../components/Layer.astro";
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
import PageLink from "../../components/PageLink.astro";
import Path from "../../components/Path.astro";
import Rule from "../../components/Rule.astro";

const APP = "web/app/src";

const H = {
  short: "Nothing moves under a press",
  rules: "Tabs, cards, buttons and the test that checks them",
  ref: "Code",
};
const toc = [
  { id: "short", label: H.short },
  { id: "rules", label: H.rules },
  { id: "ref", label: H.ref },
];
---

<Layout toc={toc}>
  <Layer n={1} title={H.short}>
    <p>
      This page answers: why doesn't an Iki page shift while it loads, and what keeps it that way?
      A press that begins on one control and is let go on another clicks neither. So a page that
      moves while it's loading loses presses, or hands them to a neighbour. Iki's pages avoid that
      in three ways: loaders read everything that decides a toolbar before the first render; parts
      that wait on reads are laid out hidden until they've settled; and what changes late above a
      control is held as it was until the press is let go (<Path p={`${APP}/steady.ts`} />). The
      entity route's loader, which reads the watch state before the page draws, is the example on{" "}
      <PageLink to="Pages and routing" hash="example" />.
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={4} title={H.rules}>
    <div class="rules">
      <Rule title="Tabs and cards that draw where they stay" open>
        <p>
          A project's and a record's tab rows draw only once every read that decides which tabs
          there are, and how wide each is, has answered; until then a quiet bar holds the place of
          each tab there's sure to be (<Path p={`${APP}/tab-strip.tsx`} />). A project's overview
          cards are laid out hidden until each reports it has settled, or a time budget runs out; a
          card that's late draws into a reserve of fixed height, so nothing around it moves (<Path
            p={`${APP}/settling.tsx`}
            sym="export function useSettled("
          />). Changing a filter or the sort keeps the grid's rows in place until the new results
          come (<Path p={`${APP}/entities.ts`} sym="placeholderData" />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Buttons that can't be pressed stay in place">
        <p>
          Buttons that can't be pressed set <code>aria-disabled</code> or <code>aria-busy</code>{" "}
          and swallow the press, rather than using the native <code>disabled</code> attribute (<Path
            p="web/packages/ui/src/button.ts"
            sym="if (unavailable) event.preventDefault();"
          />). <Inferred why="No comment or ADR says why.">That keeps them focusable and in place.</Inferred>
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="The browser tests check it">
        <p>
          A script installed in every page and frame of a browser test records each press and
          release and every layout shift; the test fails when a press and its release land on
          different targets (<Path p="server/tests/conftest.py" sym="def _watch_browser_click_stability(" />,{" "}
          <Path p="server/tests/click_stability.js" />). <PageLink to="Browser tests" /> has the rest of
          what every page is checked for.
        </p>
      </Rule>
    </div>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={5} title={H.ref}>
    <div class="refs">
      <h3>Where</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><Path p={`${APP}/steady.ts`} />: holding what changes late above a control</li>
        <li><Path p={`${APP}/settling.tsx`} />: cards laid out hidden until they settle</li>
        <li><Path p={`${APP}/tab-strip.tsx`} />: tab rows that draw once</li>
        <li><Path p="server/tests/click_stability.js" />: the press-and-release check</li>
      </ul>
      <p>How reads themselves work is on <PageLink to="Reading data" />.</p>
    </div>
  </Layer>
</Layout>
